sections.body:
`name` ko words mein search karna hai; `brand` ko exact filter/group karna hai; `price` ko numeric compare karna hai. **Mapping** batati hai har field ka type aur indexing behavior.

`text`: analysis ke baad tokens, full-text `match` ke liye. `keyword`: exact term (unless configured normalizer changes it), filters/sort/aggregations ke liye. `term` query input ko text analyzer se nahi guzarti; `text` field par raw `Samsung Galaxy` term bhejna usual full-text intent solve nahi karta.

Create index: `PUT /products-v1`
```json
{
  "settings":{"number_of_shards":3,"number_of_replicas":1},
  "mappings":{"dynamic":"strict","properties":{
    "name":{"type":"text","fields":{"raw":{"type":"keyword"}}},
    "description":{"type":"text"},
    "brand":{"type":"keyword"},
    "price":{"type":"long"},
    "in_stock":{"type":"boolean"},
    "version":{"type":"long"}
  }}
}
```
Ye **illustrative cluster setting** hai, universal recommendation nahi. Single-node local cluster mein 1 replica unassigned rahegi. `price` yahan integer paise mein hai: ₹75,000 = 7,500,000 paise. Currency alag field se model karna multi-currency app mein zaroori hai.

`dynamic: strict` unknown fields reject karta hai; controlled schema mein accidental mapping explosion se bachta hai. Existing field type badalne ke liye usually naya index aur reindex migration chahiye. [Elastic: mapping](https://www.elastic.co/docs/manage-data/data-store/mapping).

DSL matlab structured query language; yahan JSON request body. Name/description relevance mein help karein, brand/price eligibility control karein.

`POST /products-v1/_search`
```json
{
  "size":10,
  "_source":["name","brand","price","in_stock"],
  "query":{"bool":{
    "must":[{"multi_match":{
      "query":"Samsung 5G Phone",
      "fields":["name^3","description"],
      "operator":"and"
    }}],
    "filter":[
      {"term":{"brand":"Samsung"}},
      {"range":{"price":{"lte":8000000}}},
      {"term":{"in_stock":true}}
    ]
  }},
  "aggs":{"brands":{"terms":{"field":"brand","size":10}}}
}
```
`name^3` name field contribution boost karta hai; exact score 3× final score hoga ye guarantee nahi. `must` lexical matching/scoring karta hai; `filter` yes/no eligibility deta hai without relevance contribution. `lte` less-than-or-equal hai. Is request ka brands aggregation **already filtered** results par hoga; Samsung filter ke saath doosre brands ka sidebar expect mat karo.

`multi_match` default best-fields mein AND per field apply hota hai; name mein Samsung aur description mein Phone hone se dono terms automatically cross-field satisfy nahi hote. Cross-field intent ho toh compatible analyzers ke saath `cross_fields`/`combined_fields` evaluate karo. [Elastic: multi-match](https://www.elastic.co/docs/reference/query-languages/query-dsl/query-dsl-multi-match-query).

Restaurant mein chai 1 minute mein aur sandwich 5 minute mein ready hota hai. Chai ko sandwich ka wait kyun karwaana? Same idea jab learning demo PostgreSQL aur Elasticsearch results compare karti hai.

**Sequential:** PG finish, phir ES start. **Concurrent:** dono start together. **Streaming:** jo ready hua uska result client ko turant emit karo. Concurrent launch alone streaming nahi; `await Promise.allSettled(...)` ke baad dono emit karoge toh faster result bhi slower ka wait karega.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
 participant UI as Browser
 participant API as Streaming API
 participant PG as PostgreSQL
 participant ES as Elasticsearch
 UI->>API: Search request
 par Both start
 API->>PG: FTS query
 and
 API->>ES: Search DSL
 end
 ES-->>API: First result ready
 API-->>UI: Event: elasticsearch result
 PG-->>API: Second result ready or error
 API-->>UI: Event: postgres result/error
 API-->>UI: Event: done
```
Diagram mein ES first illustrative hai; PG bhi first finish kar sakta hai. Niche delays swap karke dekho.

**SSE — Server-Sent Events:** server se browser tak event messages. Wire format mein `data:` aur blank-line separator chahiye:
```text
event: source_result
data: {"source":"elasticsearch","status":"ok","items":[]}

```
Raw JSON lines ko `text/event-stream` bol dena valid SSE framing nahi. JSON Lines format chahiye toh NDJSON framing/content type choose karo aur streaming fetch reader se parse karo.

```javascript
// Conceptual server-side orchestration; sendSse adds proper framing.
const tasks = [
  ['postgres', () => pgSearch(term)],
  ['elasticsearch', () => esSearch(term)]
];
await Promise.all(tasks.map(async ([source, search]) => {
  try {
    const items = await search();
    sendSse('source_result', { source, status: 'ok', items });
  } catch {
    sendSse('source_result', { source, status: 'error' });
  }
}));
sendSse('done', {});
```
Errors per task handled hain, isliye ek fail ho toh other result ship hota hai. Production mein per-source timeout, client disconnect cancellation, request ID, permission filters aur proxy buffering handle karo. Streaming start ke baad normal HTTP status replace nahi kar sakte; per-source errors event contract mein aati hain.

Compare UI mein panels **source-labelled** rakho. Do rank scores directly compare ya lists concatenate karke “best” mat declare karo. Actual fallback sirf primary fail hone par query start kar sakti hai; har production search dono databases ko hit kare, ye requirement nahi. [MDN: SSE message framing](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Server-sent_events/Using_server-sent_events).

Millions of existing products ko one-by-one synchronous request se migrate karna slow ho sakta hai. **Bulk API** ek request mein multiple operations bhejti hai, lekin poora batch single atomic transaction nahi hota.

`POST /_bulk` with `Content-Type: application/x-ndjson`. Har action aur document separate line; body ka last newline required:
```text
{"index":{"_index":"products-v1","_id":"103"}}
{"name":"Samsung S25","brand":"Samsung","price":7500000,"in_stock":true,"version":1}
{"index":{"_index":"products-v1","_id":"104"}}
{"name":"Pixel Phone","brand":"Google","price":6000000,"in_stock":true,"version":1}
```
HTTP success ke saath kuch items fail ho sakte hain. `errors` aur har item status inspect karo; transient failed items retry, schema errors investigate/DLQ. Stable IDs aur version policy retries mein preserve karo. Batch size bytes aur latency se tune karo; giant batch memory aur retry cost badhata hai.

**Backfill** existing rows ka initial import hai. Live writes ke saath safe migration ke liye change capture start point/snapshot plan define karo. Example: consistent snapshot + recorded change-log position, snapshot load, captured changes replay with versions, verify catch-up, then cutover. Simple “SELECT all then switch” concurrent updates lose kar sakta hai.

Bulk example static learning request hai; website khud external ES ko writes nahi bhejti. [Elastic: bulk API](https://www.elastic.co/docs/api/doc/elasticsearch/operation/operation-bulk).

SDK remote engine nahi. Python/TypeScript object ko JSON request mein bhejne aur response parse karne ki convenient library hai. REST API aur SDK **same backend search** se baat karte hain.

Maan lo `reviews.csv` mein `id,review,sentiment` columns hain. Review text full-text hai; sentiment exact positive/negative tag. Explicit new mapping create karo; existing production index blindly delete mat karo:
```json
{"mappings":{"dynamic":"strict","properties":{
 "review":{"type":"text"},"sentiment":{"type":"keyword"}
}}}
```
Body ko `PUT /reviews-v1` par use karo. Credentials environment mein, server side par. Matching client/cluster major version choose karo.

```python
import csv
import os
from elasticsearch import Elasticsearch, helpers

es = Elasticsearch(os.environ['ES_URL'],
                   api_key=os.environ['ES_API_KEY'])
def actions(path):
    with open(path, newline='', encoding='utf-8') as f:
        for row in csv.DictReader(f):
            if not row.get('id') or not row.get('review'):
                continue  # real import: record rejected-row reason
            yield {
                '_index': 'reviews-v1', '_id': row['id'],
                '_source': {'review': row['review'],
                            'sentiment': row.get('sentiment', '')}
            }

for ok, item in helpers.streaming_bulk(
    es, actions('reviews.csv'), chunk_size=200,
    max_retries=3, raise_on_error=False
):
    if not ok:
        print('Investigate failed item:', item)
```
Generator ek-ek action yield karta hai; poori CSV RAM mein load nahi. Stable `_id` same review ki replay par duplicate records avoid karti hai. `streaming_bulk` per-item outcomes inspect karne deta hai; helper ke retries all failure classes ko automatically fix nahi karte. Transport exceptions, per-item permanent failures aur rejected CSV rows ko importer ke failure report/retry workflow mein handle karo.

Typing `gret product` par fuzzy query + sentiment filter same DSL se banegi. `must` text scoring; `filter` exact sentiment eligibility. SDK switch karne se query ka meaning change nahi hota. [Elastic: Python helpers](https://www.elastic.co/docs/reference/elasticsearch/clients/python/client-helpers).

Code illustrative integration hai; website local CSV upload ya live ES writes nahi karti.
